-- Avatar builder: replace hardcoded "Professional portrait" prompt with ICP-derived portrait rules.
-- The old Step 3 always generated corporate headshots regardless of persona.
-- New Step 3 forces the model to derive wardrobe, setting, mood, and photo style from persona_data.

UPDATE agent_skills
SET markdown_content = E'# Avatar Builder — Standalone Buyer Persona\n\n> Use this skill when the user wants to create a buyer persona / avatar OUTSIDE of the offer pipeline.\n> If the user is building an offer, use the offer-builder skill instead (step 3 creates the persona as part of the offer flow).\n\n## When to Use This\n\n- User says "create a buyer persona" or "build an avatar" without an active offer\n- User wants a persona for a new market segment\n- User wants to update or refine an existing persona\n- User wants a B2B ICP as a standalone deliverable\n\n## Process\n\n### Step 0: Brief the User (MANDATORY — before ANY tool call)\n\nBefore you gather data or create the persona, you MUST send a text message explaining what you''re about to build.\n\nTell them:\n\n- What type of persona (buyer persona, ICP, segment profile)\n- The approach you''ll take (what research angle, what psychological depth)\n- How it connects to their offer/business\n\nExample: "I''m going to build a deep buyer persona based on your coaching offer. I''ll map out their daily frustrations, emotional triggers, and the exact language they use when describing their problem — so every piece of copy we write after this speaks directly to them."\n\nOnly after sending this text should you proceed to Step 1.\n\n### Step 1: Gather Input\n\nYou need at minimum:\n\n- **Who is the customer?** (audience description, market)\n- **What problem do they have?** (pain points, frustrations)\n- **What solution exists?** (product/service, or general category)\n\nOptionally:\n\n- Sales page URL, competitor URL, existing marketing materials\n- Existing offer data (check `offers` table for the campaign)\n\n### Step 2: Deep Psychological Research\n\nUse the buyer persona framework below to create a dimensional, emotionally resonant profile. This is NOT a basic demographic sketch. It''s a forensic psychological investigation.\n\n### Step 3: Generate Avatar Portrait\n\nAfter building the full persona, generate a portrait image before saving. The portrait must **look like** the person you just described — do NOT default to a corporate headshot.\n\n**Rules:**\n\n- **Do not default to professional/corporate.** Only use suit-and-tie, studio backdrop, or "confident executive" framing when the persona''s career and lifestyle clearly call for it (e.g. B2B SaaS buyer, Fortune-500 exec, corporate lawyer).\n- **Derive every visual detail from `persona_data`.** Read `demographics` (career, lifestyle, daily routine), `background_profile`, `content_preferences` (tone, themes), and `comprehensive_summary` for subculture, aesthetic, and environment cues.\n- **Match wardrobe, hair, setting, and expression to the ICP.** A yoga-retreat attendee wears linen in a garden, not a blazer in a studio. A skater dad wears a graphic tee at a park, not a button-down in a coworking space.\n- **Pick a photographic style that fits the persona''s world:** documentary, lifestyle, environmental, street, festival, studio editorial — choose based on who they are, not a fixed default.\n- **For high-realism portraits** (editorial, documentary), follow the structure in `references/detailed-portrait-prompting.md`: subject + demographic, skin detail, eyes + gaze, wardrobe + cultural dress, accessories, light + environment, camera + optics.\n- Always use `1:1` aspect ratio.\n\n**Prompt structure:** `[Photographic style] portrait of a [age]-year-old [gender/appearance cues]. [Wardrobe derived from lifestyle/subculture]. [Expression matching personality]. [Setting derived from daily routine or environment]. [Lighting style]. Headshot and shoulders framing. [Aspect: 1:1]`\n\n**Contrasting examples** (adapt — do not copy verbatim):\n\n```\n# B2B SaaS marketing director\nvibey_backend({ action: "generate_image", label: "Generating avatar portrait", data: { "prompt": "Editorial headshot of a 34-year-old woman in a tailored navy blazer over a white crewneck. Confident but approachable expression, slight smile. Modern open-plan office with natural window light from the left, shallow depth of field, 85mm lens. Headshot and shoulders framing.", "aspect_ratio": "1:1" } })\n```\n\n```\n# Counterculture wellness seeker / hippie audience\nvibey_backend({ action: "generate_image", label: "Generating avatar portrait", data: { "prompt": "Lifestyle portrait of a 29-year-old man with sun-bleached shoulder-length hair and a relaxed grin. Wearing a loose linen shirt and beaded bracelet. Sitting cross-legged on a wooden deck surrounded by potted plants, warm golden-hour sunlight, soft bokeh greenery background. Documentary candid style, 50mm lens. Headshot and shoulders framing.", "aspect_ratio": "1:1" } })\n```\n\n- The response includes a `url` field — save this as `avatar_image` inside `persona_data` in the next step.\n\n### Step 4: Save to Database\n\n**See `skills/vibey-api/SKILL.md` for the full reference.** Use the `vibey_backend` tool to save data.\n\nInclude the portrait URL from Step 3 as `avatar_image` in `persona_data`:\n\n```\nvibey_backend({ action: "create_avatar", data: { "offer_id": "{OFFER_ID or null}", "name": "Rachel — The Overwhelmed Solopreneur", "avatar_type": "buyer_persona", "persona_data": { "avatar_image": "{url from Step 3}", ...complete persona JSON... } } })\n```\n\n### Step 5: Present Summary\n\nShow the user a brief, vivid snapshot of the persona — name, age, core problem, 3 key emotions, dream transformation. Under 150 words. They click the Artifacts tab for full detail.\n\n---\n\n## Buyer Persona Framework\n\nGenerate ALL of the following sections as a JSON object inside `persona_data`:\n\n### Demographics\n\n- Representative name, age, career stage, family status\n- Lifestyle, income range, daily routine\n- Key frustrations (3-5 specific ones)\n\n### Core Problem\n\nThe prospect''s deepest pain — not just a nuisance but a serious roadblock. Write 2-3 visceral sentences in the buyer''s voice.\n\n### Powerful Emotions (5)\n\nThe strongest emotions tied to the core problem, with vivid examples.\n\n- Format: "Emotion — Specific manifestation"\n- Example: "Resentment — Watching less-qualified competitors charge premium rates while she undercharges"\n\n### Biggest Fears (5)\n\nDeep, private fears — the subconscious demons. Specific and brutal.\n\n- Example: "Fear of being exposed as someone who doesn''t actually know what she''s doing"\n\n### Fear Impact on Relationships (5)\n\nHow each fear affects specific relationships (spouse, kids, friends, colleagues). Show emotional collateral damage.\n\n### Hurtful Comments (5)\n\nSpecific, stinging things close people have said that reinforce insecurities.\n\n- Example: "Her husband once said ''Maybe this business thing isn''t really working out'' during a budget conversation"\n\n### Past Attempts to Solve (5)\n\nWhat they''ve tried before and why it failed or fell short.\n\n### Avoidance Behaviors (5)\n\nThings they DON''T want to do — dreaded steps, sacrifices. Written as internal monologue.\n\n- Example: "I don''t want to become one of those hustle-culture people posting motivational quotes at 5am"\n\n### Perfect Outcomes\n\nIf a genie could snap their fingers: what does life look like? Write 2-3 visceral sentences in their voice, then list 5 specific transformational outcomes.\n\n### Transformation Impact (5)\n\nSpecific shifts in daily emotions, habits, confidence, appearance, social standing.\n\n### Success Markers\n\nThe ONE concrete milestone that symbolizes "I made it."\n\n### Secondary Gains\n\nHidden comforts from the problem they''re unwilling to sacrifice.\n\n### Blame Targets (5)\n\nExternal forces they scapegoat.\n\n### Main Objections (5)\n\nGeneral objections about the topic/industry — NOT product-specific.\n\n### Background Profile\n\nQuick life snapshot — 2-3 sentences in buyer''s voice.\n\n### Psychological Drivers\n\n- Short-term goals (days/weeks)\n- Mid-term goals (weeks/months)\n- Long-term goals (1-2+ years)\n\n### Internal Voice\n\nUnspoken thoughts, fears, phrases they repeat to themselves.\n\n### Content Preferences\n\n- Voice/tone that resonates\n- Content themes and topics\n- Emotional triggers that activate response\n- Ideal messaging tone\n\n### Comprehensive Summary\n\nCombine everything into a detailed summary. Include emotional drivers, relevant quotes, and actionable insights for writing copy that converts.\n\n---\n\n## Output Quality Standards\n\n- **Visceral, not generic.** "Fear of being exposed as a fraud at the next networking event" not "fears failure"\n- **Specific, not vague.** "Pays $295/mo across 6 marketing tools" not "spends money on tools"\n- **In their voice.** Use their language, their internal monologue, their way of describing problems\n- **Evidence-based.** Pull from the offer research, market data, or web research. Don''t invent.\n- **Dimensional.** This is a real person with contradictions, guilt, pride, and secret desires.\n\n---\n\n## Rules\n\n- **ALWAYS save to DB** via the avatars table\n- **Link to offer_id** if the avatar comes from an offer''s step 3\n- **Pull from offer data** when available — don''t research from scratch if step 3 already exists\n- **NEVER mention** databases, APIs, JSON, or technical details to the user\n- Present the persona as a vivid character, not a data dump\n\n\n---\n\n## Ecology\n\nThe avatar is the psychological foundation that makes every other asset resonate:\n\n- **Depends on:** offer-builder (if an offer exists, step 3 already contains the buyer persona — use it instead of researching from scratch)\n- **Feeds into:** funnel-builder (pain points become section headlines, transformation becomes the hero promise), email-sequence-builder (persona language drives subject lines and copy voice), lead-magnet-builder (pain points determine content topics), ad-builder (fears and desires become ad hooks), social-content-builder (content themes come from persona interests)\n\nWithout a defined avatar, every skill generates for a generic audience. With one, every piece of copy speaks directly to one person — and that specificity is what converts.',
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE skill_key = 'avatar-builder'
  AND agent_key = 'vibey'
  AND user_id IS NULL;

-- Also update any global (agent_key = '*') row if it exists
UPDATE agent_skills
SET markdown_content = (
  SELECT markdown_content FROM agent_skills
  WHERE skill_key = 'avatar-builder' AND agent_key = 'vibey' AND user_id IS NULL
  LIMIT 1
),
    updated_at = NOW()
WHERE skill_key = 'avatar-builder'
  AND agent_key = '*'
  AND user_id IS NULL;
